{
  "id": "174ea705-2e06-4c52-a185-4c2ca2998026",
  "page_title": "How to work with prompt engineering and the Chat Completion API",
  "page_description": "Learn how to work with the Chat Completion API for prompt engineering.",
  "page_date": "02/16/2024",
  "chunk_title": "Add clear syntax",
  "chunk_content": "Add clear syntax  Using clear syntax for your prompt—including punctuation, headings, and section markers—helps communicate intent and often makes outputs easier to parse.  In the example below, separators (`---` in this case) have been added between different sources of information or steps. This allows the use of `---` as a stopping condition for generation. In addition, section headings or special variables are presented in uppercase to differentiate them.  | System message |User     | Assistant  | |----------------|---------|------------| | You're an AI assistant that helps people find information.| You'll read a paragraph, and then issue queries to a search engine in order to fact-check it. Also explain the queries.<br>---<br>PARAGRAPH<br>John Smith is married to Lucy Smith. They have five kids, and he works as a software engineer at Microsoft. What search queries should I do to fact-check this?<br>---<br>QUERIES|1. \"John Smith Microsoft\" - To check if John Smith is indeed employed at Microsoft.<br>2. \"John Smith Lucy Smith\" - To check if John Smith is married to Lucy Smith.<br>3. \"John Smith children\" - To check if John Smith has five children.|  If you're not sure what syntax to use, consider using Markdown or XML. The models have been trained on a large quantity web content in XML and Markdown, which might provide better results.",
